{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0\fs24 Seed Quotation Checklist: Eight Commercial Terms to Confirm Before Approval \u183? Questions and answers\par Seed quotations often mix several separate commercial fields into one document, which makes them easy to misread as a single offer or as confirmation of supply. This FAQ-style guide breaks the review into practical questions so a reseller can check each field before approving a supply arrangement. It focuses on the commercial terms of a seed quotation and does not replace the quotation itself or any subsequent agreement.\par \b Does sending an enquiry mean a purchase is confirmed?\b0\par No. Adding products to an enquiry basket and submitting the form does not conclude a purchase contract. An automated acknowledgement only confirms that the enquiry was received for review. The commercial terms of a proposed purchase are set out in the quotation and any subsequent agreement. Read the quotation as a set of separate commercial fields rather than a single price or an automatic promise of supply. The site's terms of service explain how enquiries, quotations and supply terms differ, and that distinction should frame the whole review.\par \b Which fields should be checked line by line?\b0\par Match the variety and requested quantity against the quotation, and treat catalogue entries and listed prices as no proof of current stock or of supply to a particular destination. Record the pack size and the unit used for the quantity, because a price per kilogram is a different basis from a price for a stated number of seeds. Identify the price actually quoted rather than the catalogue price, and keep applicable taxes, shipping charges, documentation costs and payment terms as their own fields. Confirm responsibilities for documents, customs clearance, duties and taxes, then check the destination country and postal code against the enquiry and quotation. If a delivery date was requested, label it as requested rather than promised.\par \b How should unresolved conditions be tracked?\b0\par Give every commercial item a visible status instead of relying on a general note such as quotation received. A practical status list is: confirmed in the quotation, agreed separately, awaiting supplier response, awaiting reseller approval, and not applicable. Add the affected variety, field and destination to each open point. Do not close a field merely because an automated acknowledgement arrived, and do not infer availability from a catalogue entry. If a condition is unanswered, request it in writing and keep it marked pending until it appears in the quotation or a subsequent agreement.\par \b What is a practical pre-approval sequence?\b0\par Create one row per variety with requested quantity, pack size, unit, quoted price, availability and destination, which makes omissions visible and prevents weight-based figures from being compared with seed counts. Add separate columns for taxes, shipping charges, documentation costs, payment terms, delivery arrangements, required documents and import responsibilities. Compare the quotation with the destination, the requested delivery date and documentation needs, keeping the requested date separate from a confirmed delivery commitment. Resolve pending fields in writing and record whether each answer appears in the quotation or the subsequent agreement. Complete the internal review only after each field is confirmed, marked not applicable or recorded as an explicit exception.\par \b Why keep price, pack size and unit apart?\b0\par A listing price is not a substitute for the review of pack size, available quantity and final price in the quotation. When weight-based and seed-count-based figures are compared without their recorded units, the comparison itself becomes unreliable. Keeping pack size, unit and price in separate columns forces the reviewer to state the basis of each figure and exposes missing information before approval rather than after. The same logic applies to taxes and shipping charges: treating a listed price as the complete commercial amount hides costs that the quotation should specify separately.\par \b What remains true after the review?\b0\par Reviewing the fields separately makes omissions visible and keeps unresolved conditions from being lost inside a general acknowledgement. It also preserves the essential distinction between starting a review and settling its terms: an enquiry starts the review, while the quotation and subsequent agreement record the supply terms. Approving a supply arrangement on the strength of an enquiry alone, or on a catalogue entry, leaves open questions about availability, documentation and delivery that the written record has not yet answered.\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulkseedsinternational-en-a2eff0.gitlab.io/seed-quotation-commercial-terms-checklist/"}{\fldrslt Seed Quotation Checklist: Eight Commercial Terms to Confirm Before Approval}}\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulk-seeds-international.github.io/bulkseedsinternational-en/dateien/minimax-8893955acc8ae9c833d387a7/inhalt.odt"}{\fldrslt Related document}}\par }